.TH NVME_TP_MONITOR 1 "September 2026" "ebpf-syscall" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nvme_tp_monitor \- capture NVMe driver commands and completion latency
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nvme_tp_monitor
.RB [ \-\-dur " SECONDS" ]
.RB [ \-\-jsonl " PATH" ]
.RB [ \-\-disk " NVME_NAMESPACE" ]
.RB [ \-\-lba-size " BYTES" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nvme_tp_monitor
attaches to Linux NVMe command and completion tracepoints.  It records the
ordered operation, namespace, LBA, byte length, monotonic issue time, hardware
queue, command ID, completion status, and completion latency.  A final drop
record lets consumers detect capture loss.
.PP
This is the device witness used by
.BR "kvio record" .
It sees commands from every submission API that reaches the selected NVMe
driver, including IO not issued by kvio.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-dur " seconds"
Stop after the given duration.  Otherwise stop with SIGINT or SIGTERM.
.TP
.BI \-\-jsonl " path"
Write newline-delimited JSON to the path instead of standard output.
.TP
.BI \-\-disk " namespace"
Capture only the named block namespace, for example
.BR nvme1n1 .
The monitor discovers its logical block size from sysfs.  Filtering is
required for an unambiguous portable replay capture.
.TP
.BI \-\-lba-size " bytes"
Supply a positive power-of-two logical block size.  This is required when
.B \-\-disk
is omitted because several observed namespaces can have different geometry.
.SH OUTPUT AND PRIVACY
Command records contain IO metadata, not payload bytes, file names, KV object
keys, graph nodes, prompts, or tensors.  Completion records contain status,
latency, queue, command ID, and time.
.PP
Payload-free does not mean anonymous.  Exact LBAs reveal locality and device
range, timing reveals request cadence, and disk identity or an unusual shape
can identify a system or workload.  Minimize and review captures before
sharing them.
.SH FIDELITY
Require a final
.B dropped: 0
record for an exact-stream claim.  Use
.B kvio iolog
to export the requested stream, then capture the fio replay with this monitor
and use
.B kvio compare
to check ordered operations, byte offsets and lengths, issue timing, and
completion-latency distributions.
.PP
Completion records do not carry disk identity.  Consumers pair them using
hardware queue, command ID, and time ordering or use their latency
distribution.  Current kvio comparison does not establish a one-to-one latency
pair between an original command and its replay.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kvio (1),
.BR nvme_uring_cmd_monitor (1),
.BR iouring_monitor (1)
